---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import SolarPanels from '@/components/tools/calc2/SolarPanels.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="solar-panel-calculator">
  <SolarPanels client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How the solar panel calculator works</h2>
    <p>
      The solar panel calculator sizes a grid-tied system to cover your electricity use over a year. It
      divides your daily use by the peak sun hours and by the system efficiency to get the size in kW,
      then by the panel rating to get the number of panels.
    </p>
    <pre><code>system size (kW) = daily use (kWh) ÷ (peak sun hours × derate)
panels           = ⌈system size × 1,000 ÷ panel watts⌉
yearly output    = installed kW × peak sun hours × derate × 365</code></pre>

    <h2>Worked example</h2>
    <p>
      A home that uses 30 kWh a day in a place with 5 peak sun hours, at 80% efficiency, needs
      30 ÷ (5 × 0.8) = 7.5 kW. With 400 W panels that is ⌈7,500 ÷ 400⌉ = 19 panels, about 37 m² of roof.
    </p>

    <h2>Peak sun hours</h2>
    <p>
      Peak sun hours are the day's sunlight on a tilted panel, given as hours at 1,000 W/m². Most of the
      United States gets 3.5 to 6, northern Europe 2.5 to 3.5 as a yearly average, and deserts more than 6.
      Use a yearly average for your location; winter values are much lower.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      The result covers your use on average over the year, not every day. Shade, roof direction and tilt
      change the output. Batteries and off-grid systems need extra capacity for cloudy days.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
